BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/676451.jpg
CATEGORIES:Online Talk,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260805T055202Z
DESCRIPTION:Rama and Lakshmana with her sage Vishvamitra from the " Shangri" Ramayana\, c. 1700.\nNorthern India\, Pahari kingdoms.\nThe “Shangri” Ramayana has intrigued scholars and collectors for over a century. Created around 1700 for a royal patron in the Pahari kingdoms\, this illustrated manuscript is among the most ambitious visual retellings of the Ramayana. Yet\, despite its significance\, its origins and production have long remained a mystery.\n\nIn this talk\, art historian Sonya Rhie Mace presents research based on previously unpublished paintings that reshapes our understanding of the manuscript. Her findings reveal a far greater number of folios and artistic styles than previously recognised\, suggesting that the manuscript was created through a collaborative network of artists working across the Pahari region\, spanning present-day Himachal Pradesh and Jammu &amp\; Kashmir. The talk also traces how this once-unified manuscript became dispersed across collections over time\, and how new discoveries are helping to reconstruct both its visual narrative and the artistic networks behind its creation. Join us to explore these illustrated manuscripts and the new perspectives it offers on Pahari painting and artistic collaboration.\n\nThis programme at MAP is part of A Closer Look\, a monthly series of research-focused talks by artists\, curators and art historians focusing on visual cultures of South Asia.
DTEND:20260812T140000Z
DTSTART:20260812T130000Z
SUMMARY:Curating a Dispersed Epic
